      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here. This curve is y equals cos two x, and by the chain rule, its derivative is minus two sin two x. At x equals pi over six, that gives a gradient of minus root three. Picture the curve as a road on a map, with a car at that point. At night, its headlights shine straight along the tangent. So is that enough to draw the tangent? Not quite. Here's a line with gradient minus root three. Here's another, and another. They're all parallel, and only one of them passes through the point on the curve. A gradient tells you the direction of a line, but not where it sits. So how do a point and a derivative pin down the tangent, and the line through the same point at right angles to it, straight across the road, called the normal?"
    },
